## Builder Pattern

## Intent

Separate the construction of a complex object from its representation, using the same process to produce different results.

## Fluent Builder (Most Common in Frontend)

```typescript
class QueryBuilder {
  private query = {
    table: '',
    fields: ['*'] as string[],
    conditions: [] as string[],
    orderField: null as string | null,
    limitValue: null as number | null,
  };

  from(table: string): this {
    this.query.table = table;
    return this; // enables chaining
  }

  select(...fields: string[]): this {
    this.query.fields = fields;
    return this;
  }

  where(condition: string): this {
    this.query.conditions.push(condition);
    return this;
  }

  orderBy(field: string): this {
    this.query.orderField = field;
    return this;
  }

  limit(n: number): this {
    this.query.limitValue = n;
    return this;
  }

  build(): string {
    const { table, fields, conditions, orderField, limitValue } = this.query;
    if (!table) throw new Error('Table is required');
    let sql = `SELECT ${fields.join(', ')} FROM ${table}`;
    if (conditions.length) sql += ` WHERE ${conditions.join(' AND ')}`;
    if (orderField) sql += ` ORDER BY ${orderField}`;
    if (limitValue) sql += ` LIMIT ${limitValue}`;
    return sql;
  }
}

const sql = new QueryBuilder()
  .from('users')
  .select('id', 'name', 'email')
  .where('active = true')
  .where('age > 18')
  .orderBy('name')
  .limit(50)
  .build();
// SELECT id, name, email FROM users WHERE active = true AND age > 18 ORDER BY name LIMIT 50
```

## Test Data Factory (Builder for Tests)

```typescript
// Without builder — repetitive, fragile test setup
const user: User = {
  id: '1', name: 'Aaron', email: 'aaron@example.com',
  role: 'user', active: true, createdAt: new Date(),
};

// With builder — only override what's relevant to the test
class UserBuilder {
  private user: User = {
    id: '1',
    name: 'Test User',
    email: 'test@example.com',
    role: 'user',
    active: true,
    createdAt: new Date('2024-01-01'),
  };

  withRole(role: User['role']): this { this.user.role = role; return this; }
  inactive(): this { this.user.active = false; return this; }
  withEmail(email: string): this { this.user.email = email; return this; }
  build(): User { return { ...this.user }; }
}

// In tests — only specify what matters for this test
const adminUser = new UserBuilder().withRole('admin').build();
const inactiveUser = new UserBuilder().inactive().build();
```

## URL Builder

```typescript
class UrlBuilder {
  private base: string;
  private path = '';
  private params = new URLSearchParams();

  constructor(base: string) { this.base = base; }

  withPath(path: string): this { this.path = path; return this; }
  withParam(key: string, value: string): this { this.params.set(key, value); return this; }

  build(): string {
    const query = this.params.toString();
    return `${this.base}${this.path}${query ? '?' + query : ''}`;
  }
}

const url = new UrlBuilder('https://api.example.com')
  .withPath('/products')
  .withParam('category', 'electronics')
  .withParam('page', '2')
  .build();
// https://api.example.com/products?category=electronics&page=2
```

## When to Use

```
✅ Object has many optional parameters (avoids telescoping constructors)
✅ Construction process should be reusable across representations
✅ Test data — override only what's relevant per test
✅ Fluent query/config APIs
❌ Object is simple — Builder adds unnecessary overhead
```

## Dependency Injection vs Service Locator

## Dependency Injection

Dependencies are passed in — the class/function declares what it needs and the caller provides it.

```typescript
// DI via constructor — dependencies are visible in the signature
class OrderService {
  constructor(
    private readonly orderRepo: OrderRepository,
    private readonly emailService: EmailService,
    private readonly paymentProcessor: PaymentProcessor
  ) {}

  async placeOrder(items: CartItem[], userId: string): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.orderRepo.create({ items, userId });
    await this.paymentProcessor.charge(order.total);
    await this.emailService.sendConfirmation(userId, order);
    return order;
  }
}

// At the call site — you see exactly what OrderService needs
const orderService = new OrderService(
  new SupabaseOrderRepository(),
  new SendGridEmailService(),
  new StripeProcessor()
);

// In tests — inject mocks, no magic
const testService = new OrderService(
  mockOrderRepo,
  mockEmailService,
  mockPaymentProcessor
);
```

## Service Locator

A global registry — classes pull their dependencies from it.

```typescript
// Service Locator implementation
class ServiceLocator {
  private static services = new Map<string, unknown>();

  static register<T>(token: string, service: T): void {
    this.services.set(token, service);
  }

  static resolve<T>(token: string): T {
    const service = this.services.get(token);
    if (!service) throw new Error(`Service not found: ${token}`);
    return service as T;
  }
}

// Register at startup
ServiceLocator.register('orderRepo', new SupabaseOrderRepository());
ServiceLocator.register('emailService', new SendGridEmailService());

// Class pulls from the locator — dependencies are hidden
class OrderService {
  private orderRepo = ServiceLocator.resolve<OrderRepository>('orderRepo');
  private emailService = ServiceLocator.resolve<EmailService>('emailService');

  async placeOrder(items: CartItem[], userId: string) {
    // Callers have NO IDEA this class depends on orderRepo and emailService
  }
}
```

## Why DI Wins

```
DI:
  ✅ Dependencies visible in the signature — self-documenting
  ✅ Easy to test — just pass mocks
  ✅ No global state — parallel tests don't interfere
  ✅ Compiler catches missing dependencies

Service Locator:
  ❌ Hidden dependencies — you can't tell what a class needs without reading it
  ❌ Hard to test — must configure the global locator before each test
  ❌ Runtime failures — wrong token string → crashes at runtime, not compile time
  ❌ Global state — tests can pollute each other
```

## DI in React (the Idiomatic Way)

```typescript
// Props = DI for components
function UserCard({ user, onEdit }: { user: User; onEdit: (id: string) => void }) {
  return <div onClick={() => onEdit(user.id)}>{user.name}</div>;
}

// Context = DI for subtrees
const AuthContext = createContext<AuthContextValue | null>(null);

// Custom hooks = DI for logic
function useUserService(repo: UserRepository = defaultUserRepo) {
  return {
    getUser: (id: string) => repo.findById(id),
  };
}
// In tests: useUserService(mockRepo)
```

## When Service Locator is Acceptable

```
✅ Framework internals (React's reconciler uses internal registries)
✅ Plugin systems where the consumer doesn't know the plugins upfront
✅ Large apps with many layers where threading dependencies becomes impractical
   (but prefer a DI framework over a hand-rolled locator)
```
